Default implementation of interface
members
We all know that, in C#, interfaces don't have any method implementations; they only
contain the method signature. In C# 8, however, interfaces are allowed to have
implemented methods. These methods can be overridden by classes if they need to be.
Interface methods will also have access to modifiers, such as public, virtual, protected, or
internal. By default, the access level is set to virtual unless it is fixed as sealed or private.

